  {
    "pkattachmentId": 36572,
    "bookid": 34,
    "chapterId": 2,
    "fileId": 16662,
    "type": "Figure",
    "diagnosis": "Typical immunohistochemical profile of the 5&nbsp;principal histotypes of ovarian carcinoma",
    "title": "",
    "legend": "<strong>First row:</strong>&nbsp;High-grade serous carcinomas (HGSCs) express WT1 diffusely in almost all cases; however, occasional cases exhibit focal or negative staining. This, in combination with mutation-type p53 staining, is highly specific for HGSC and can be used for confirmation of a morphological diagnosis. Mutation-type p53 expression (associated with <em>TP53</em> mutation) refers to overexpression (strong nuclear expression involving &gt;&nbsp;80% of tumour cell nuclei); less commonly, complete absence of expression in tumour cell nuclei with retained internal control; or, rarely, unequivocal cytoplasmic expression. Napsin&nbsp;A expression is absent in HGSC, and only a minority of cases express PR. <strong>Second row:</strong>&nbsp;Low-grade serous carcinomas (LGSCs) express WT1 in virtually all cases, in combination with wildtype p53 expression; the latter is characterized by variable staining distribution and intensity in tumour cell nuclei. Napsin&nbsp;A is absent and PR is expressed in most cases. <strong>Third row:</strong>&nbsp;Endometrioid carcinomas (ECs) are usually WT1-negative (note that 10&ndash;14% can express WT1, some in a diffuse manner) and usually show wildtype p53 expression. Although a minority of ECs exhibit mutation-type p53 staining, the combination of WT1 staining and mutation-type p53 staining occurs only rarely. ECs do not express napsin&nbsp;A (with rare exceptions), but PR is expressed in a large majority of cases. <strong>Fourth row:</strong>&nbsp;Clear cell carcinomas (CCCs) almost never express WT1, and they show wildtype p53 staining in the vast majority of cases. Napsin&nbsp;A is frequently positive, although only focal in a subset, which can lead to a false negative result on small biopsies. PR is usually absent. <strong>Fifth row:</strong>&nbsp;Mucinous carcinomas (MCs) are virtually always WT1-negative. Many cases exhibit mutation-type p53 staining. Napsin&nbsp;A and PR are almost always negative.",
    "source": "K&ouml;bel M",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "",
    "imageUrl": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/34/2/16662.jpg",
    "thumbnail": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/34/2/thumb_16662.jpg"
  },
